export const c17SystemsProjectReading: Record<string, string> = {
	"CSE4-Memory-Visualizer":
		"### Required project: Memory Visualizer for Arrays and Structs\n\nConfirm the learner import to edit and save the project in the site IDE. Complete the named functions in starter/layout.c. Download and extract the ZIP for native compilation. Save the attempt before opening the separate reference. Each import includes only the selected role and the unchanged complete guide.\n\nThis required memory-and-layout project contrasts object lifetimes and\nmeasures record layout. Complete a padding analyzer, then add a double field\nin a separate experimental record and report the changed layout. Explain\nwhat the measurements establish and which details belong to the compiler.\n\nUse the C17 build and translation-unit workflow from the opening units.\nThese labs target Clang or GCC on macOS, Linux or WSL2 with 8-bit bytes,\nand require CMake 3.20 or newer. No additional libraries are needed.\n\nEdit and save the selected role in the site editor when its confirmed import\nis available. Download and extract that role's ZIP before native compilation.\nKeep the learner attempt before opening the separate reference.\n\n## Storage and addresses\n\nThe static banner has static storage duration. The local record, integer array\nand pointer variable have automatic duration. The object obtained through\nmalloc has allocated duration until free. Automatic storage is commonly\nimplemented on a stack and allocated storage on a heap; the language's\nlifetime categories are the useful distinction.\n\nThe pointer variable and the allocated object are different objects.\n&allocated_record addresses the pointer variable; allocated_record holds the\naddress of the allocated record. Compare both printed lines. Allocation can\nfail, so the supplied driver checks for NULL before using the result and\nfrees the allocated object once.\n\nPrinted addresses describe this execution. They can change between runs.\nNo fixed numeric ordering of unrelated static, automatic and allocated\nobjects is part of the project contract.\n\nWithin sample_window, &sample_window[1] - &sample_window[0] is an index gap\nof one. Array elements are contiguous, so the byte stride is sizeof(int).\nUse the same-array pointer difference and the type size; do not subtract\nunrelated object addresses or infer a byte count from differently typed pointers.\n\n## Fields, alignment and padding\n\nFrameRecord contains, in declaration order, a char tag, uint32_t sample_count,\nuint16_t error_code and char status. offsetof reports each field's offset\nfrom the record start; sizeof reports its width and the total record size;\n_Alignof reports the record's alignment requirement.\n\nFor each field:\n\n~~~text\ngap_before = field_offset - previous_field_end\nfield_end  = field_offset + field_width\ntail       = record_size - last_field_end\npadding    = sum(gap_before) + tail\nrecord_size = sum(field_width) + padding\n~~~\n\nAlignment can introduce gaps between fields and tail padding. These are\nlayout measurements, not initialized data bytes to interpret. This driver\nreports their sizes without reading padding contents. The exact offsets and\nalignment depend on the implementation; obtain them from the compiler.\n\nOn the validated local Clang run, the base record had offsets 0, 4, 8 and 10,\nsize 12, alignment 4, four padding bytes and one tail-padding byte. This is\nan observed example, not a fixed ABI assumption. Another layout can pass the\nsame contracts with different measurements.\n\n## Files and two implementation tasks\n\nEach role includes main.c (supplied storage/address demonstration), layout.h\n(contracts and report types), layout.c (tasks or reference), tests.c and\nCMakeLists.txt. The unchanged complete README accompanies the selected role.\n\n1. Implement analyze_layout in layout.c. Reject null arguments, zero or more\n   than eight spans, a zero object size, a nonzero first offset, zero-width\n   members, overlaps and members past the object end. Check subtraction bounds\n   before addition. On invalid input return LAYOUT_INVALID and leave output\n   unchanged. For valid spans calculate all gaps, tail, member bytes and total\n   padding, then commit a complete LayoutReport and return LAYOUT_OK.\n2. Implement describe_experiment. Declare a local record with the original\n   four field types/order plus double calibration. Obtain all five offsets,\n   widths, total size and alignment with offsetof, sizeof and _Alignof. Fill\n   ExperimentLayout and call analyze_layout for its report. Reject null output;\n   otherwise commit only a successful complete experiment.\n\nThe LAYOUT_UNFINISHED value is a learner marker, distinct from invalid input.\nKeep the supplied driver, interface and contracts unchanged while completing\nthese tasks. The separate reference includes both the analyzer and added-field\nexperiment. Predict the new double field's alignment effect before comparing.\n\n## Build, run and expected unfinished behavior\n\nFrom the directory containing this README and the selected role:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S starter -B build-starter -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-starter\nctest --test-dir build-starter --output-on-failure\n./build-starter/cse4_memory_visualizer_starter\n~~~\n\nThe untouched learner compiles, but its contract test fails and the normal\nprogram invocation exits 78 with an unfinished diagnostic and no report.\nComplete the named tasks, rebuild, then rerun the tests and program.\nSave that attempt before opening the separate reference:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S solution -B build-solution -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-solution\nctest --test-dir build-solution --output-on-failure\n./build-solution/cse4_memory_visualizer_solution\n~~~\n\nReplace clang with gcc for GCC. On Windows use the corresponding .exe.\nA sanitizer configuration adds -DCSE_SANITIZERS=ON. Also configure a separate\nbuild with -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release; contract checks stay active there.\nBoth role folders build independently. Invalid command syntax exits 2.\n\n## Read, predict, implement, check and explain\n\nRecord predictions before execution: which line addresses the pointer\nvariable, which line addresses its allocation, the array index gap, each\nfield's width and which gaps might appear. Run the program and reconcile each\nprediction with its actual output. Change neither the expected addresses nor\nthe platform into hardcoded constants.\n\nThe contracts cover 200 synthetic layouts, invalid/overflow-shaped spans,\nunchanged outputs on failure and the compiler-measured five-field experiment.\nA packed-layout build in the native validation changes the ABI measurements;\nthe implementation must still derive them correctly.\n\nCompletion evidence: passing learner contracts, both layout reports, a\ncorrect explanation of pointer variable versus allocation, the added-field\ncomparison and the equations accounting for every record byte. A guided\nwalkthrough uses the same predictions and measurements.\n\nFor further practice, change one field type or its position in an optional\ncopy, update its descriptors and predict the new measurements. Explain the\ntradeoff before treating a smaller record as automatically preferable.\n\nLanguage reference: WG14 C draft N1570, sections 6.2.4, 6.5.6 and 6.7.2.1:\nhttps://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n1570.pdf",
	"CSE5-Byte-Buffer-Workbench":
		'### Required project: Byte Buffer Workbench\n\nConfirm the learner import to edit and save the project in the site IDE. Complete the named functions in starter/buffers.c. Download and extract the ZIP for native compilation. Save the attempt before opening the separate reference. Each import includes only the selected role and the unchanged complete guide.\n\nThis required strings-and-buffers project distinguishes a terminated string\nprefix, the complete binary payload and destination capacity. Complete a\nbounded copy and bounded terminator scan, then explain their different jobs.\n\nUse the C17 build and translation-unit workflow from the opening units.\nThese labs target Clang or GCC on macOS, Linux or WSL2 with 8-bit bytes,\nand require CMake 3.20 or newer. No additional libraries are needed.\n\nEdit and save the selected role in the site editor when its confirmed import\nis available. Download and extract that role\'s ZIP before native compilation.\nKeep the learner attempt before opening the separate reference.\n\n## Length, capacity and meaningful bytes\n\nA C string ends at its first zero byte. Its string length counts the bytes\nbefore that terminator. Capacity counts all available destination positions,\nincluding space for the terminator. A binary payload instead carries an\nexplicit count; a zero byte can be meaningful data within that payload.\n\nThe default label sensor-A1 contains nine bytes. A destination of eight bytes\ncan preserve at most seven label bytes plus its terminator, so the reported\nlabel is sensor-, length 7, capacity 8, with a truncated copy status. The\nshort label ok fits and the unused destination bytes are zero-filled.\n\n~~~text\nlabel bytes:       73 65 6E 73 6F 72 2D 00\nshort label bytes: 6F 6B 00 00 00 00 00 00\nraw packet bytes: 4F 4B 00 21 7F 10 20 30\n~~~\n\nThe packet contains a valid terminated two-byte prefix, OK, followed by more\npayload bytes. String scanning would measure that prefix as 2; it would not\nmeasure the complete eight-byte packet. A separate four-byte span ABCD has\nno terminator at all. The supplied driver reports that fact after a bounded\nscan and never calls an unbounded string function on that span.\n\nCopying operates on bytes. A truncated UTF-8 label can end inside a multibyte\ncharacter. The hex dump identifies what was copied; this exercise does not\nclaim to preserve Unicode character boundaries.\n\n## Files, contracts and two tasks\n\nEach role includes main.c (supplied examples), buffers.h, buffers.c, tests.c\nand CMakeLists.txt. The complete README remains outside the role directory.\n\n1. Complete copy_label in buffers.c. Source must be a terminated string and\n   must not overlap destination; destination must contain capacity bytes.\n   Reject null source/destination and zero capacity without changing output.\n   Copy at most capacity-1 bytes, terminate and zero-fill every remaining\n   destination byte. Return COPY_COMPLETE if all source bytes fit, otherwise\n   COPY_TRUNCATED. An empty string fits even in a one-byte destination.\n2. Complete find_terminator. Scan only the declared span. Return BUFFER_OK and\n   the first zero index when found. Return BUFFER_NO_TERMINATOR without\n   changing the length output when absent. A null pointer with capacity zero\n   is an empty span; null data with a nonzero count or null output is invalid.\n\nThe unfinished markers are distinct from normal results. Keep signatures,\nsupplied examples and contracts unchanged. Do not use strlen to discover\nwhether a raw span contains a terminator: the bounded scan establishes that\nprecondition first.\n\n## Build, run and expected unfinished behavior\n\nFrom the directory containing this README and the selected role:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S starter -B build-starter -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-starter\nctest --test-dir build-starter --output-on-failure\n./build-starter/cse5_byte_buffer_workbench_starter\n~~~\n\nThe untouched learner compiles, but its contract test fails and the normal\nprogram invocation exits 78 with an unfinished diagnostic and no report.\nComplete the named tasks, rebuild, then rerun the tests and program.\nSave that attempt before opening the separate reference:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S solution -B build-solution -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-solution\nctest --test-dir build-solution --output-on-failure\n./build-solution/cse5_byte_buffer_workbench_solution\n~~~\n\nReplace clang with gcc for GCC. On Windows use the corresponding .exe.\nA sanitizer configuration adds -DCSE_SANITIZERS=ON. Also configure a separate\nbuild with -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release; contract checks stay active there.\nBoth role folders build independently. Invalid command syntax exits 2.\n\n## Inputs and predictions\n\nUse zero arguments for sensor-A1 or one argument for a custom label. Quote\nspaces in a POSIX shell. Extra arguments are invalid.\n\n~~~sh\n./build-solution/cse5_byte_buffer_workbench_solution "ok"\n./build-solution/cse5_byte_buffer_workbench_solution ""\n./build-solution/cse5_byte_buffer_workbench_solution "12345678"\n~~~\n\n| Source and capacity | Prediction |\n| --- | --- |\n| empty string, capacity 1 | complete; one zero byte |\n| x, capacity 1 | truncated; empty terminated destination |\n| seven-byte label, capacity 8 | complete; terminator at index 7 |\n| eight-byte label, capacity 8 | truncated to seven bytes |\n| packet with first zero at index 2 | string prefix 2; payload remains 8 |\n| four nonzero bytes | no terminator within that span |\n\nThe contracts cover 561 independent length/capacity combinations, guard bytes\noutside each destination, zero-fill behavior, invalid/null arguments and\nunchanged length outputs on failure. Release builds retain those checks.\n\nCompletion evidence: passing contracts, predicted byte dumps, an explanation\nof the packet\'s terminated prefix versus payload count, and evidence that the\nunterminated example is never passed to an unbounded string function. For a\nguided walkthrough, inspect one capacity boundary before implementing each loop.\n\nOptional extension: define a Unicode-aware copy policy in a separate exercise,\nor add a counted byte-copy helper. State its overlap and truncation contracts;\ndo not silently change this byte-oriented string helper.',
	"CSE6-Packet-Serializer-Deserializer":
		"### Required project: Packet Serializer Deserializer\n\nConfirm the learner import to edit and save the project in the site IDE. Complete the named functions in starter/packet.c. Download and extract the ZIP for native compilation. Save the attempt before opening the separate reference. Each import includes only the selected role and the unchanged complete guide.\n\nThis required serialization project turns typed fields into an explicit\nseven-byte record and validates that representation before reconstructing a\npacket. The following optional file-reader project applies a separate binary\nformat to repeated FILE-based records.\n\nUse the C17 build and translation-unit workflow from the opening units.\nThese labs target Clang or GCC on macOS, Linux or WSL2 with 8-bit bytes,\nand require CMake 3.20 or newer. No additional libraries are needed.\n\nEdit and save the selected role in the site editor when its confirmed import\nis available. Download and extract that role's ZIP before native compilation.\nKeep the learner attempt before opening the separate reference.\n\n## The version-1 wire format\n\nA struct's padding and alignment belong to its implementation. Serializing\nits raw object representation would also depend on host byte order. This\nproject defines each byte directly instead.\n\n| Offset | Field | Contract |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | magic | C3 hexadecimal |\n| 1 | version | 1; other versions are rejected |\n| 2 | channel | one unsigned byte, 0..255 |\n| 3 | flags | one unsigned byte, 0..255 |\n| 4 | reading low byte | reading_mv modulo 256 |\n| 5 | reading high byte | reading_mv divided by 256 |\n| 6 | checksum | XOR of bytes 0..5 |\n\nThe reading is unsigned 0..65535. Decode it as low + 256*high. This\nlittle-endian format is independent of the host's byte order and sizeof the\nPacket struct. The checksum detects many accidental changes; balanced XOR\nchanges can cancel, so it does not authenticate a packet.\n\nFor version 1, channel 2, flags 5 and reading 3300, predict:\n\n~~~text\nC3 01 02 05 E4 0C 2D\n~~~\n\nE4 is low byte 228 and 0C is high byte 12; 228 + 12*256 = 3300.\nXOR the first six bytes to obtain 2D.\n\n## Files and two tasks\n\nEach role contains main.c (supplied checked CLI), packet.h, packet.c,\ntests.c and CMakeLists.txt, accompanied by this complete README. The supplied\nchecksum helper is available inside packet.c.\n\n1. Complete serialize_packet. Reject null packet/buffer/used output, capacity\n   below seven and unsupported versions. Invalid inputs preserve both buffer\n   bytes and the previous used value. Build the seven explicit bytes, write\n   only those bytes and then set used to seven. Never write a raw struct.\n2. Complete parse_packet. Check for non-null input/output and exactly seven\n   bytes. Reject wrong magic, unsupported version or checksum mismatch before\n   modifying output. Decode the little-endian reading into a temporary Packet,\n   then commit a complete successful result.\n\nReturn PACKET_OK, PACKET_INVALID or the untouched learner's\nPACKET_UNFINISHED marker. Keep the public signatures, driver and tests intact.\nThe reference implements the same format and failure-preservation rules.\n\n## Build, run and expected unfinished behavior\n\nFrom the directory containing this README and the selected role:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S starter -B build-starter -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-starter\nctest --test-dir build-starter --output-on-failure\n./build-starter/cse6_packet_serializer_starter\n~~~\n\nThe untouched learner compiles, but its contract test fails and the normal\nprogram invocation exits 78 with an unfinished diagnostic and no report.\nComplete the named tasks, rebuild, then rerun the tests and program.\nSave that attempt before opening the separate reference:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S solution -B build-solution -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-solution\nctest --test-dir build-solution --output-on-failure\n./build-solution/cse6_packet_serializer_solution\n~~~\n\nReplace clang with gcc for GCC. On Windows use the corresponding .exe.\nA sanitizer configuration adds -DCSE_SANITIZERS=ON. Also configure a separate\nbuild with -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release; contract checks stay active there.\nBoth role folders build independently. Invalid command syntax exits 2.\n\n## Checked CLI and boundary predictions\n\nZero arguments use reading 3300. One argument chooses a reading using whole\nASCII decimal digits or 0x/0X plus hexadecimal digits, in 0..65535. Decimal\nleading zeroes remain decimal: 077 means 77. Signs, spaces, incomplete prefixes,\ntrailing characters and overflow are errors; extra arguments are rejected.\nThese CLI checks are supplied, so invalid CLI input exits 2.\n\n~~~sh\n./build-solution/cse6_packet_serializer_solution 0\n./build-solution/cse6_packet_serializer_solution 0xFFFF\n./build-solution/cse6_packet_serializer_solution 65536\n~~~\n\n| Reading | Low/high prediction |\n| --- | --- |\n| 0 | 00 00 |\n| 255 | FF 00 |\n| 256 | 00 01 |\n| 65535 | FF FF |\n| 65536 or 12junk | invalid CLI; no packet report |\n\nThe native contracts check all 65,536 readings against an independent byte\nlayout and arithmetic XOR oracle, bytes beyond the serialized span, every\nsingle-byte corruption, unsupported version with a recomputed checksum,\nwrong lengths/null arguments and unchanged outputs on rejection.\n\nCompletion evidence: passing learner contracts, the default byte prediction,\ncustom low/high boundaries, rejected packets and an explanation of why a\nvalidated explicit representation differs from a raw struct dump. The same\nread/predict/implement/check/explain sequence supports a guided lesson.\n\nOptional extension: introduce a new documented protocol version with an\nadditional field, distinct length and appropriate validation. Keep version 1\nworking; do not silently reinterpret existing records.",
	"CSE7-Dynamic-Ring-Buffer":
		"### Required project: Dynamic Ring Buffer\n\nConfirm the learner import to edit and save the project in the site IDE. Complete the named functions in starter/ring.c. Download and extract the ZIP for native compilation. Save the attempt before opening the separate reference. Each import includes only the selected role and the unchanged complete guide.\n\nThis required ownership project implements a FIFO queue with allocated storage.\nUnit 7 focuses on allocation, cleanup and failure-preserving operations. Unit 9\nreturns to the saved implementation for invariants, operation traces and\ncomplexity reasoning. It continues the same project rather than requiring a\nnew unrelated container.\n\nUse the C17 build and translation-unit workflow from the opening units.\nThese labs target Clang or GCC on macOS, Linux or WSL2 with 8-bit bytes,\nand require CMake 3.20 or newer. No additional libraries are needed.\n\nEdit and save the selected role in the site editor when its confirmed import\nis available. Download and extract that role's ZIP before native compilation.\nKeep the learner attempt before opening the separate reference.\n\n## State, ownership and indexing\n\nStart with struct RingBuffer buffer = {0}. A destroyed buffer has a null data\npointer and zero capacity, head and count. An initialized buffer owns its data\nallocation and has positive capacity, head < capacity and count <= capacity.\nOnly ring_destroy releases that allocation. Borrowed data pointers must not\nbe freed separately and can become invalid after successful growth.\n\nhead is the physical position of the oldest value. count is the number of\ninitialized logical values. The next insertion position is the logical tail.\nAn empty initialized queue keeps its allocation; it differs from a destroyed\nbuffer, which must be initialized before use.\n\n~~~text\nlogical position i -> (head + i) modulo capacity\n0 <= count <= capacity\n0 <= head < capacity\n~~~\n\nThe supplied ring_index expresses this mapping without overflowing head+i.\nOnly logical positions 0..count-1 contain queue values; other slots must not\nbe read as initialized integers.\n\n## Growth and allocation failure\n\nA full queue grows to twice its capacity, after checking the multiplication\nagainst SIZE_MAX / sizeof(int). The supplied growth helper keeps realloc's\nreturn in a temporary pointer. Failure preserves the original allocation,\nstate and queue values. Success invalidates old borrowed pointers.\n\nAt growth time all old slots contain values because the queue is full. The\nhelper rotates those initialized values into logical order using three\nreversals, then commits the grown pointer, capacity and head zero. This avoids\nreading unused slots and keeps FIFO order after wrapped growth.\n\nFor capacity 2: push 1, push 2, pop 1, push 3 produces logical [2,3] with\nwrapped physical storage. Push 4 grows and produces logical [2,3,4]. Trace\nboth logical order and physical head positions before checking the program.\n\n## Files and three learner tasks\n\nEach role contains main.c; ring.h/ring.c; allocation.h/allocation.c;\ntest_allocation.h/test_allocation.c; tests.c; and CMakeLists.txt. This complete\nREADME is shared support. The program uses the normal malloc/realloc/free\nprovider. Contract tests compile the same ring.c with a supplied allocation\nprovider that can fail a chosen call and check ownership. They do not replace\nthe ring implementation or disable its guards.\n\n1. Complete ring_init. Reject null output, a buffer that is not zero/destroyed,\n   zero capacity and sizes beyond the checked allocation bound. Obtain storage\n   through cse_allocate. On failure leave state unchanged. On success commit\n   the pointer, capacity, head zero and count zero.\n2. Complete ring_push. Check for valid active state. Grow a full queue through\n   the supplied helper and propagate failure without writing a value. Append\n   at its logical tail and increment count only after storage is available.\n3. Complete ring_pop. Check for active valid state and non-null output. Empty\n   queues return RING_EMPTY and preserve output. Otherwise remove the oldest\n   value, advance/wrap head, decrease count and commit the popped value.\n\nUse RING_OK, RING_EMPTY, RING_INVALID, RING_NO_MEMORY and the untouched\nRING_UNFINISHED marker accurately. Keep the supplied allocator, growth,\ndestruction, driver and tests intact. Initialization requires a defined zero\nbuffer; passing an uninitialized struct is outside the interface contract.\n\n## Build, run and expected unfinished behavior\n\nFrom the directory containing this README and the selected role:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S starter -B build-starter -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-starter\nctest --test-dir build-starter --output-on-failure\n./build-starter/cse7_dynamic_ring_buffer_starter\n~~~\n\nThe untouched learner compiles, but its contract test fails and the normal\nprogram invocation exits 78 with an unfinished diagnostic and no report.\nComplete the named tasks, rebuild, then rerun the tests and program.\nSave that attempt before opening the separate reference:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S solution -B build-solution -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-solution\nctest --test-dir build-solution --output-on-failure\n./build-solution/cse7_dynamic_ring_buffer_solution\n~~~\n\nReplace clang with gcc for GCC. On Windows use the corresponding .exe.\nA sanitizer configuration adds -DCSE_SANITIZERS=ON. Also configure a separate\nbuild with -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release; contract checks stay active there.\nBoth role folders build independently. Invalid command syntax exits 2.\n\n## Default operation trace\n\nThe program accepts no arguments. It initializes capacity 4, pushes\n10,20,30,40,50,60, pops 10 and pushes 70. Predict this final report:\n\n~~~text\ncount: 6\ncapacity: 8\nhead: 1\npopped: 10\ncontents: 20 30 40 50 60 70\n~~~\n\nThe supplied driver checks every operation and destroys its allocation before\nreturning from an operation failure. It prints a report only after all steps\nsucceed. Destroy is safe for NULL, a zero buffer and repeated destruction of a\npreviously valid buffer; it resets ownership fields to zero.\n\nContracts use a separate contiguous-array FIFO model for 2,000 mixed\noperations, wrapped growth, empty-output preservation, invalid capacities,\nfailed initial allocation, failed reallocation, reset/reinitialization and\nlive-allocation counts. Release checks remain active.\n\n## Unit 9 continuation: maintained invariants\n\nOpen the saved Unit 7 learner attempt. Record a new trace of at least twelve\nmixed pushes/pops that forces wrapping, emptiness and growth. For each step\nwrite capacity, head, count and logical contents, then explain why the state\ninvariants still hold. Add the sequence to a personal copy of the tests.\n\nPredict a failed allocation at a full queue: which pointer, values and output\nmust remain unchanged? Compare the supplied failure-provider contract with\nyour prediction. Explain why assigning realloc directly to the owned pointer\nwould lose the old allocation on failure.\n\nA normal push/pop is constant work. Growth moves all current values; doubling\nspreads that occasional cost over many pushes. State both the individual\nresize cost and the amortized append reasoning. Compare this representation\nwith a linked list for contiguous traversal, allocation overhead and removal.\n\nCompletion evidence: passing learner contracts, cleanup evidence, the default\nFIFO report, the new annotated trace, a failure-preservation explanation and\nUnit 9's invariant/complexity comparison. These are distinct learning outputs\nfrom the same maintained program and support an instructor walkthrough.\n\nOptional extension: add a bounded-capacity policy in a separate copy with an\nexplicit full-queue result. Document and test that policy without changing\nthe growable queue's existing contract.",
	"CSE8-Fixed-Size-Log-File-Reader":
		"### Optional transfer project: Fixed Size Log File Reader\n\nConfirm the learner import to edit and save the project in the site IDE. Complete the named functions in starter/log.c. Download and extract the ZIP for native compilation. Save the attempt before opening the separate reference. Each import includes only the selected role and the unchanged complete guide.\n\nThis optional file-parsing challenge reads existing binary records, validates\nthem and prints a summary only after the complete input passes. It follows\nthe packet representation work and adds FILE-based repeated record handling.\n\nUse the C17 build and translation-unit workflow from the opening units.\nThese labs target Clang or GCC on macOS, Linux or WSL2 with 8-bit bytes,\nand require CMake 3.20 or newer. No additional libraries are needed.\n\nEdit and save the selected role in the site editor when its confirmed import\nis available. Download and extract that role's ZIP before native compilation.\nKeep the learner attempt before opening the separate reference.\n\n## Eight-byte record format\n\n| Offset | Field | Representation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0..3 | timestamp | uint32, least-significant byte first |\n| 4 | level | unsigned byte, 0..255 |\n| 5 | sensor | unsigned byte, 0..255 |\n| 6 | reading | unsigned byte, 0..255 |\n| 7 | checksum | XOR of bytes 0..6 |\n\nEach record is exactly eight bytes. There is no hidden struct padding,\nversion byte or terminator. An empty input is a valid zero-record log.\nA partial final record is rejected rather than silently counted as success.\nThe checksum catches single-byte changes, but equal bit changes in two bytes\ncan cancel. It detects many accidental errors and provides no authentication.\n\nThe supplied sample has:\n\n~~~text\nE9 03 00 00 01 07 2A C6\nF0 03 00 00 02 07 2C DA\n~~~\n\nE9 + 3*256 = timestamp 1001; F0 + 3*256 = 1008. Predict both checksums and\nall fields before running. Decoding uses explicit shifts/unsigned types,\nnot a cast from bytes to a struct.\n\n## Files, tasks and supplied I/O\n\nEach role contains main.c (supplied checked FILE workflow), log.h, log.c,\ntests.c and CMakeLists.txt. The complete README accompanies that role.\n\n1. Complete log_checksum in log.c. XOR the declared byte span and return\n   0..255. NULL with count zero is a valid empty span with checksum zero;\n   NULL with a positive count returns UINT_MAX. UINT_MAX is also the\n   untouched learner's unfinished marker and is outside byte results.\n2. Complete decode_log_record. Check for non-null input/output and exactly eight\n   bytes. Validate checksum before modifying output. Decode the timestamp,\n   copy the three byte fields into a temporary LogRecord and commit only on\n   success. Invalid input leaves previous output fields unchanged.\n\nThe supplied scanner distinguishes EOF from ferror, rejects partial records,\nchecks writes and closes, and stages the report in a temporary stream. Bad\ninput produces a diagnostic and no partial stdout report. Reading an existing\npath never creates a sample or opens that path for writing.\n\n## Build, run and expected unfinished behavior\n\nFrom the directory containing this README and the selected role:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S starter -B build-starter -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-starter\nctest --test-dir build-starter --output-on-failure\n./build-starter/cse8_fixed_log_reader_starter\n~~~\n\nThe untouched learner compiles, but its contract test fails and the normal\nprogram invocation exits 78 with an unfinished diagnostic and no report.\nComplete the named tasks, rebuild, then rerun the tests and program.\nSave that attempt before opening the separate reference:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S solution -B build-solution -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-solution\nctest --test-dir build-solution --output-on-failure\n./build-solution/cse8_fixed_log_reader_solution\n~~~\n\nReplace clang with gcc for GCC. On Windows use the corresponding .exe.\nA sanitizer configuration adds -DCSE_SANITIZERS=ON. Also configure a separate\nbuild with -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release; contract checks stay active there.\nBoth role folders build independently. Invalid command syntax exits 2.\n\n## Demo, real input and explicit sample creation\n\nZero arguments demonstrate the supplied two-record fixture through temporary\nstreams, without creating a persistent file. One argument reads an existing\npath in binary mode. --sample plus a new path explicitly creates the supplied\nfixture using exclusive creation and refuses any existing path.\n\n~~~sh\n./build-solution/cse8_fixed_log_reader_solution\n./build-solution/cse8_fixed_log_reader_solution --sample lesson.logbin\n./build-solution/cse8_fixed_log_reader_solution lesson.logbin\n~~~\n\nThe --sample operation is a supplied preparation helper; it can succeed in the\nuntouched learner. Normal demo/read invocations remain unfinished until both\nfunctions work. Reusing an existing --sample destination fails and preserves\nits bytes. Quote paths containing spaces.\n\nSuccessful default output:\n\n~~~text\nfixed-size log reader\ntimestamp=1001 level=1 sensor=7 reading=42 checksum=C6\ntimestamp=1008 level=2 sensor=7 reading=44 checksum=DA\nvalid records: 2\n~~~\n\nMalformed command syntax exits 2. File, checksum, truncation or output errors\nexit 1. Diagnostics identify how many records were validated before rejection.\nThe supplied source input remains unchanged on both success and failure.\n\n## Boundary checks and completion evidence\n\nPredict then test: empty file; one valid record; two records; a changed byte;\na seven-byte final record; a valid record plus one trailing byte; a missing\npath; and an existing sample destination. Check input byte identity after\nsuccessful and failed reads. Never demonstrate these cases by overwriting a\nsource log with a sample.\n\nThe contracts cover 256 independent record patterns, 2,048 single-byte\ncorruptions, exact lengths/null outputs and previous output preservation.\nThey also demonstrate an undetectable balanced XOR change so the stated\nchecksum limit is testable. Native CLI tests cover real files and failures.\n\nCompletion evidence: passing learner contracts, correct field/checksum\npredictions, unchanged input files, rejected corruption/truncation, explicit\nsample creation and an explanation of EOF versus read failure. An instructor\ncan walk through the same eight-byte record and FILE loop.\n\nOptional extension: add a documented magic/version envelope and a stronger\naccidental-error check. Define migration and compatibility rules rather than\nsilently changing the existing eight-byte format.",
	"CSE9-Capstone-Telemetry-Transform-CLI":
		"### Required project: Capstone Telemetry Transform CLI\n\nConfirm the learner import to edit and save the project in the site IDE. Complete the named functions in starter/telemetry.c. Download and extract the ZIP for native compilation. Save the attempt before opening the separate reference. Each import includes only the selected role and the unchanged complete guide.\n\nThis required capstone validates supplied telemetry, performs fixed-point\ntransforms and emits a normalized CSV. It combines earlier representation,\nwhole-input parsing, range checks, numerical reasoning and FILE-based work.\nExisting input is opened for reading; samples are created only on explicit\nrequest, and normal output requires a new path.\n\nUse the C17 build and translation-unit workflow from the opening units.\nThese labs target Clang or GCC on macOS, Linux or WSL2 with 8-bit bytes,\nand require CMake 3.20 or newer. No additional libraries are needed.\n\nEdit and save the selected role in the site editor when its confirmed import\nis available. Download and extract that role's ZIP before native compilation.\nKeep the learner attempt before opening the separate reference.\n\n## Project CSV grammar\n\nThe first line is exactly:\n\n~~~text\nsample_id,temp_milli_c,voltage_mv,flags\n~~~\n\nEach data line contains exactly four whole ASCII decimal fields separated by\ncommas. There are no quotes, spaces, comments, fractions, exponents, hexadecimal\nfields or additional columns in this project format. This is a defined CSV\nsubset, not a general CSV parser. Decimal leading zeroes are allowed.\nOnly temperature can have a leading minus; plus signs are rejected.\n\n| Field | Inclusive range |\n| --- | --- |\n| sample_id | 0..255 |\n| temp_milli_c | -40000..125000 |\n| voltage_mv | 0..5000 |\n| flags | 0..255 |\n\nThe supplied line reader accepts LF or CRLF and a final line without a\nnewline. It removes a terminal CR, rejects embedded null bytes and consumes\nan entire overlong line before reporting failure. A logical line can contain\nat most 127 bytes after removing its line ending. Empty data lines are invalid.\nA header-only input is valid and has zero records.\n\n## Fixed-point transform and status\n\nA value of 21500 means 21.500 degrees Celsius. Compute Fahrenheit milli-units\nwithout converting the source to floating point:\n\n~~~text\ntemp_milli_f = truncate_toward_zero(temp_milli_c * 9 / 5) + 32000\n~~~\n\nValidate the Celsius range before arithmetic and use a wide intermediate.\nThe output range is -40000..257000. Division discards a fractional milli-unit\nrather than rounding to nearest; for -1 milli-Celsius the result is 31999\nmilli-Fahrenheit. Explain this small rounding effect explicitly.\n\nStatus is cold below 0 milli-Celsius, hot above 60000 and normal otherwise.\nExactly 0 and 60000 are normal. Flags remain one byte and print as two\nhexadecimal digits; this utility preserves their value rather than claiming\nto implement an encryption or other flag-controlled behavior.\n\n## Files and two tasks\n\nEach role contains main.c (supplied complete file workflow), telemetry.h,\ntelemetry.c, tests.c and CMakeLists.txt, plus this complete README.\n\n1. Implement parse_telemetry_row. Use the supplied decimal_field helper to\n   consume all four fields and enforce their ranges. Reject null arguments,\n   empty/overlong spans, malformed or extra fields and any trailing text.\n   Construct a temporary TelemetryRow, then commit output only on success.\n2. Implement transform_telemetry_row. Reject null arguments and invalid\n   temperature/voltage ranges before arithmetic. Preserve previous output\n   on rejection. Copy the validated input fields, calculate Fahrenheit with\n   a wide intermediate and classify status, then commit the complete result.\n\nReturn TELEMETRY_OK, TELEMETRY_INVALID or the learner's\nTELEMETRY_UNFINISHED marker. Keep the interface, supplied bounded field reader,\nfile driver and contracts unchanged while completing these two functions.\n\n## Build, run and expected unfinished behavior\n\nFrom the directory containing this README and the selected role:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S starter -B build-starter -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-starter\nctest --test-dir build-starter --output-on-failure\n./build-starter/cse9_capstone_transform_starter\n~~~\n\nThe untouched learner compiles, but its contract test fails and the normal\nprogram invocation exits 78 with an unfinished diagnostic and no report.\nComplete the named tasks, rebuild, then rerun the tests and program.\nSave that attempt before opening the separate reference:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S solution -B build-solution -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-solution\nctest --test-dir build-solution --output-on-failure\n./build-solution/cse9_capstone_transform_solution\n~~~\n\nReplace clang with gcc for GCC. On Windows use the corresponding .exe.\nA sanitizer configuration adds -DCSE_SANITIZERS=ON. Also configure a separate\nbuild with -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release; contract checks stay active there.\nBoth role folders build independently. Invalid command syntax exits 2.\n\n## Demo and existing files\n\nZero arguments read the supplied fixture through temporary streams and print\nnormalized CSV directly. This demo creates no persistent input/output files.\nTwo positional arguments read an existing input and create a new output.\n--sample plus a new path explicitly creates the supplied input fixture.\n\n~~~sh\n./build-solution/cse9_capstone_transform_solution\n./build-solution/cse9_capstone_transform_solution --sample lesson-input.csv\n./build-solution/cse9_capstone_transform_solution lesson-input.csv lesson-output.csv\n~~~\n\nThe --sample helper is supplied and already works.\nNormal demo/transform invocations exit 78 until both tasks work. Malformed CLI\nsyntax exits 2; input, validation, path and I/O failures exit 1.\n\nThe driver validates every input row into temporary output before creating a\nnamed destination. Bad input leaves the source unchanged and creates no output.\nAn existing destination, the input path itself or an existing sample path is\nrefused. Failed writes remove only the newly created incomplete destination.\nThis prevents using sample generation as a hidden replacement for validation.\n\nThe normalized schema and default rows are:\n\n~~~text\nsample_id,temp_milli_c,temp_milli_f,voltage_mv,flags_hex,status\n7,21500,70700,3300,0x05,normal\n8,19875,67775,3250,0x01,normal\n9,25000,77000,3400,0x81,normal\n~~~\n\nNamed transforms report the row count after the output is closed successfully.\nLine diagnostics count the header as line 1 and the first data row as line 2.\n\n## Predictions, checks and completion evidence\n\nTest both range endpoints, -1/0/1, 60000/60001, flags 0/255, invalid 256,\nvoltage 5000/5001, a missing header, extra/trailing columns, signed unsigned\nfields, very long numbers, embedded null bytes and an overlong line. Try a\nbad second row after one good row and confirm that no partial output appears.\nCheck source bytes after every real-file case. Test an existing output and\ninput/output equality without changing the original files.\n\nContracts cover every valid integer Celsius milli-value from -40000 through\n125000: 165,001 independently checked transforms, status boundaries,\ncomplete-field grammar, invalid arguments and output preservation. Native\nchecks additionally verify real file handling, LF/CRLF, final lines, failures,\nexplicit sample creation and named output preservation in Debug and Release.\n\nCompletion evidence: passing learner contracts, correct fixed-point\npredictions, all three default normalized rows, rejected bad input with line\nnumbers, byte-identical source files and no output created on invalid input.\nThe same steps form an instructor walkthrough: predict, parse, validate,\ntransform, check the file and explain the failure boundary.\n\nOptional extension: define one new derived field or validation policy in a\nseparate copy. Document its units, range and compatibility; keep the original\nschema and tests working while demonstrating the extension.",
	ringContinuation:
		"### Required continuation: maintain the Unit 7 ring buffer\n\nReopen the saved Unit 7 implementation rather than starting another queue. Keep its ownership and failure-preservation behavior, then extend the evidence with the Unit 9 twelve-operation trace, invariants and operation-cost comparison below. This card retains separate lesson progress and a separate return link while opening the same saved program. Save the learner attempt before inspecting the reference.\n\nThis required ownership project implements a FIFO queue with allocated storage.\nUnit 7 focuses on allocation, cleanup and failure-preserving operations. Unit 9\nreturns to the saved implementation for invariants, operation traces and\ncomplexity reasoning. It continues the same project rather than requiring a\nnew unrelated container.\n\nUse the C17 build and translation-unit workflow from the opening units.\nThese labs target Clang or GCC on macOS, Linux or WSL2 with 8-bit bytes,\nand require CMake 3.20 or newer. No additional libraries are needed.\n\nEdit and save the selected role in the site editor when its confirmed import\nis available. Download and extract that role's ZIP before native compilation.\nKeep the learner attempt before opening the separate reference.\n\n## State, ownership and indexing\n\nStart with struct RingBuffer buffer = {0}. A destroyed buffer has a null data\npointer and zero capacity, head and count. An initialized buffer owns its data\nallocation and has positive capacity, head < capacity and count <= capacity.\nOnly ring_destroy releases that allocation. Borrowed data pointers must not\nbe freed separately and can become invalid after successful growth.\n\nhead is the physical position of the oldest value. count is the number of\ninitialized logical values. The next insertion position is the logical tail.\nAn empty initialized queue keeps its allocation; it differs from a destroyed\nbuffer, which must be initialized before use.\n\n~~~text\nlogical position i -> (head + i) modulo capacity\n0 <= count <= capacity\n0 <= head < capacity\n~~~\n\nThe supplied ring_index expresses this mapping without overflowing head+i.\nOnly logical positions 0..count-1 contain queue values; other slots must not\nbe read as initialized integers.\n\n## Growth and allocation failure\n\nA full queue grows to twice its capacity, after checking the multiplication\nagainst SIZE_MAX / sizeof(int). The supplied growth helper keeps realloc's\nreturn in a temporary pointer. Failure preserves the original allocation,\nstate and queue values. Success invalidates old borrowed pointers.\n\nAt growth time all old slots contain values because the queue is full. The\nhelper rotates those initialized values into logical order using three\nreversals, then commits the grown pointer, capacity and head zero. This avoids\nreading unused slots and keeps FIFO order after wrapped growth.\n\nFor capacity 2: push 1, push 2, pop 1, push 3 produces logical [2,3] with\nwrapped physical storage. Push 4 grows and produces logical [2,3,4]. Trace\nboth logical order and physical head positions before checking the program.\n\n## Files and three learner tasks\n\nEach role contains main.c; ring.h/ring.c; allocation.h/allocation.c;\ntest_allocation.h/test_allocation.c; tests.c; and CMakeLists.txt. This complete\nREADME is shared support. The program uses the normal malloc/realloc/free\nprovider. Contract tests compile the same ring.c with a supplied allocation\nprovider that can fail a chosen call and check ownership. They do not replace\nthe ring implementation or disable its guards.\n\n1. Complete ring_init. Reject null output, a buffer that is not zero/destroyed,\n   zero capacity and sizes beyond the checked allocation bound. Obtain storage\n   through cse_allocate. On failure leave state unchanged. On success commit\n   the pointer, capacity, head zero and count zero.\n2. Complete ring_push. Check for valid active state. Grow a full queue through\n   the supplied helper and propagate failure without writing a value. Append\n   at its logical tail and increment count only after storage is available.\n3. Complete ring_pop. Check for active valid state and non-null output. Empty\n   queues return RING_EMPTY and preserve output. Otherwise remove the oldest\n   value, advance/wrap head, decrease count and commit the popped value.\n\nUse RING_OK, RING_EMPTY, RING_INVALID, RING_NO_MEMORY and the untouched\nRING_UNFINISHED marker accurately. Keep the supplied allocator, growth,\ndestruction, driver and tests intact. Initialization requires a defined zero\nbuffer; passing an uninitialized struct is outside the interface contract.\n\n## Build, run and expected unfinished behavior\n\nFrom the directory containing this README and the selected role:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S starter -B build-starter -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-starter\nctest --test-dir build-starter --output-on-failure\n./build-starter/cse7_dynamic_ring_buffer_starter\n~~~\n\nThe untouched learner compiles, but its contract test fails and the normal\nprogram invocation exits 78 with an unfinished diagnostic and no report.\nComplete the named tasks, rebuild, then rerun the tests and program.\nSave that attempt before opening the separate reference:\n\n~~~sh\ncmake -S solution -B build-solution -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug\ncmake --build build-solution\nctest --test-dir build-solution --output-on-failure\n./build-solution/cse7_dynamic_ring_buffer_solution\n~~~\n\nReplace clang with gcc for GCC. On Windows use the corresponding .exe.\nA sanitizer configuration adds -DCSE_SANITIZERS=ON. Also configure a separate\nbuild with -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release; contract checks stay active there.\nBoth role folders build independently. Invalid command syntax exits 2.\n\n## Default operation trace\n\nThe program accepts no arguments. It initializes capacity 4, pushes\n10,20,30,40,50,60, pops 10 and pushes 70. Predict this final report:\n\n~~~text\ncount: 6\ncapacity: 8\nhead: 1\npopped: 10\ncontents: 20 30 40 50 60 70\n~~~\n\nThe supplied driver checks every operation and destroys its allocation before\nreturning from an operation failure. It prints a report only after all steps\nsucceed. Destroy is safe for NULL, a zero buffer and repeated destruction of a\npreviously valid buffer; it resets ownership fields to zero.\n\nContracts use a separate contiguous-array FIFO model for 2,000 mixed\noperations, wrapped growth, empty-output preservation, invalid capacities,\nfailed initial allocation, failed reallocation, reset/reinitialization and\nlive-allocation counts. Release checks remain active.\n\n## Unit 9 continuation: maintained invariants\n\nOpen the saved Unit 7 learner attempt. Record a new trace of at least twelve\nmixed pushes/pops that forces wrapping, emptiness and growth. For each step\nwrite capacity, head, count and logical contents, then explain why the state\ninvariants still hold. Add the sequence to a personal copy of the tests.\n\nPredict a failed allocation at a full queue: which pointer, values and output\nmust remain unchanged? Compare the supplied failure-provider contract with\nyour prediction. Explain why assigning realloc directly to the owned pointer\nwould lose the old allocation on failure.\n\nA normal push/pop is constant work. Growth moves all current values; doubling\nspreads that occasional cost over many pushes. State both the individual\nresize cost and the amortized append reasoning. Compare this representation\nwith a linked list for contiguous traversal, allocation overhead and removal.\n\nCompletion evidence: passing learner contracts, cleanup evidence, the default\nFIFO report, the new annotated trace, a failure-preservation explanation and\nUnit 9's invariant/complexity comparison. These are distinct learning outputs\nfrom the same maintained program and support an instructor walkthrough.\n\nOptional extension: add a bounded-capacity policy in a separate copy with an\nexplicit full-queue result. Document and test that policy without changing\nthe growable queue's existing contract."
};
